        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Car Insurance Rates</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the difference between full coverage and minimum liability? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            State minimum liability only pays for injuries and property damage you cause to other people in a crash. It pays $0 toward your own car repairs or medical bills. Full coverage adds Collision (crash damage to your own vehicle) and Comprehensive (theft, hail, fire, vandalism, and animal strikes) so you aren't stuck paying out of pocket to replace your car.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Will checking car insurance rates hurt my credit score? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. Insurance quotes use a "soft inquiry" on your credit file. Soft pulls never lower your FICO or VantageScore and don't show up to mortgage or auto lenders.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">How far back do car insurance companies look at my driving record? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Most insurers check the last 3 to 5 years (36 to 60 months) of your driving record for tickets and at-fault crashes. Major offenses like DUIs can stay on your insurance pricing for 5 to 7 years, depending on state rules.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can an insurance company raise my rates if an accident wasn’t my fault? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            States like California and Oklahoma bar insurers from surcharging you for a crash you didn't cause. Elsewhere—especially in no-fault states—filing a claim under your own PIP or collision coverage can still cost you a claims-free discount or bump you into a higher risk tier at renewal.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

